# Agentic workflows: how coding agents work, and where you can steer them

*This is the written version of the core part of my talk in the Agentic Coding Workflows course at JSC. I first wrote it in August 2026 and updated it in November 2026 to follow the current slides and the example scripts in the [course repository](https://gitlab.jsc.fz-juelich.de/sdlaml/agentic-coding/agentic-coding-workflows).*

## [You type one sentence. What happens next?]($heading.id('you-type-one-sentence-what-happens-next'))

Here is a typical exchange with a coding agent such as Claude Code:

```
> fix the failing test

• Bash(pytest -x)
  └ FAILED tests/test_auth.py::test_login
    fixture 'client' not found
• Read(tests/conftest.py)
• Edit(tests/conftest.py)  +6 lines
• Bash(pytest -x)
  └ 24 passed in 1.8s

The client fixture was never defined,
so pytest could not inject it. I added
it to tests/conftest.py; all tests pass.
```

You typed one sentence, and the agent ran the tests twice, read a file, edited it and explained the fix. Four questions hide in that exchange:

1. **The model.** How did it pick each word?
2. **The context.** How did it know your code?
3. **The loop.** How did it run `pytest`?
4. **The harness.** Who let it edit files?

Each question belongs to one layer, and the layers nest. The model sits at the centre. The context is everything the model sees. The loop lets the model act. The harness is the program wrapped around all of it. This post walks outward from the centre, one layer at a time.

[The four layers of a coding agent. Each section of this post covers one ring.](<$image.asset('fig-layers.svg').alt('Four concentric rings labelled model, context, loop and harness')>)

## [1. The model: how is the next token chosen?]($heading.id('the-model-how-is-the-next-token-chosen'))

### [A language model predicts the next token]($heading.id('a-language-model-predicts-the-next-token'))

A language model is a program trained on huge amounts of text and code. Given the text so far, it predicts what comes next. It produces one token at a time: each new token is appended to the text, and the model runs again on the longer text.

### [Tokens: the units a model reads and writes]($heading.id('tokens-the-units-a-model-reads-and-writes'))

A **token** is a chunk of text that the model treats as one unit, and each token maps to a number. Common words are often a single token. Rarer words, identifiers and punctuation are split into several.

[How a few strings might be split into tokens.](<$image.asset('fig-tokens.svg').alt('Four strings split into coloured token chunks, with token counts of 1, 4, 4 and 7')>)

A common rule of thumb is that one token is about four characters of English prose [[1]](https://help.openai.com/en/articles/4936856-what-are-tokens-and-how-to-count-them). Code needs more tokens per character, because symbols, brackets and camelCase identifiers split into small pieces. Each model family also has its own tokenizer, so the same file can have different counts in different models.

### [How the next token is picked]($heading.id('how-the-next-token-is-picked'))

At every step the model gives each token in its vocabulary a score (a *logit*) [`z_i`]($mathtex). A softmax turns the scores into probabilities, and one token is **sampled** from them. The **temperature** [`T`]($mathtex) rescales the scores first:

```=mathtex
p_i = \frac{e^{z_i / T}}{\sum_j e^{z_j / T}}
```

At [`T = 1`]($mathtex) you get the model's own distribution. A lower temperature gives the top token more of the probability, and as [`T \to 0`]($mathtex) the top token always wins (*greedy decoding*). A higher temperature flattens the distribution, so unlikely tokens are picked more often.

The figure applies this to the prompt *"The animal that barks is the ___"* with five candidate tokens.

[Temperature reshapes the same distribution: lower is more focused, higher gives more surprises.](<$image.asset('fig-temperature.svg').alt('Three bar charts of next-token probabilities for dog, cat, wolf, fox and car at temperatures 0.5, 1 and 2')>)

At [`T = 0.5`]($mathtex), `dog` gets 88% of the probability. At [`T = 2`]($mathtex) it drops to 39%, and even `car` is picked about one time in nine.

The right temperature depends on the task:

| Task | Temperature | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Code generation, bug fixing | 0 | The most probable, consistent answer |
| Code review | 0 to 0.3 | Surfaces a few different issues |
| Documentation | 0.3 to 0.5 | Some natural variety |
| Brainstorming | 0.7 to 1.0 | Diverse ideas |

### [Top-p and top-k: drop the tail, then sample]($heading.id('top-p-and-top-k-drop-the-tail-then-sample'))

Temperature reshapes the whole distribution. Two other settings cut off its unlikely **tail** before sampling:

- **Top-p** (nucleus sampling) [[2]](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.09751) keeps the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to at least [`p`]($mathtex). It keeps few tokens when the model is confident and more when it is unsure.
- **Top-k** [[3]](https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.04833) always keeps the [`k`]($mathtex) most likely tokens, however the probability is spread.

The surviving tokens are renormalized and one is sampled. In the figure, `car` is never drawn under either rule.

[Top-p = 0.9 keeps dog, cat, wolf and fox, since together they reach 0.95. Top-k = 2 keeps only dog and cat.](<$image.asset('fig-topp-topk.svg').alt('Three stacked horizontal bars showing which tokens top-p 0.9 and top-k 2 keep')>)

### [Model settings in practice]($heading.id('model-settings-in-practice'))

| Setting | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `temperature` | 1.0 | 0 for code. Change this or `top_p`, not both |
| `top_p` | 1.0 (off) | 0.9 to 0.95 for controlled variety |
| `top_k` | off | Rarely exposed by coding tools |
| `max_tokens` | none | Hard cap on the length of the response |
| `stop_sequences` | none | Strings that end generation early |

Coding tools such as Claude Code and Cursor choose these values for you, so you meet them only when you call an API yourself. Even then, a clearer prompt changes the output far more than any of them. Newer reasoning models reject the sampling settings altogether and take an **effort** level instead, which controls how much the model thinks before it answers [[4]](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/effort).

### [Why tokens matter]($heading.id('why-tokens-matter'))

Tokens are the unit of both cost and capacity.

- **You pay per token.** Input and output are billed separately, and output costs several times more. Pasting a whole file to fix one function wastes input tokens, and asking for a full rewrite where a small edit would do wastes output tokens.
- **The context window is a fixed budget.** The system prompt, the tool definitions, your messages, the files and the tool output all share it. Because the model is stateless, the whole conversation is sent again on every request, so long sessions fill the window fast. The next section explains why.

## [2. The context: what does the model see?]($heading.id('the-context-what-does-the-model-see'))

### [The context window is working memory]($heading.id('the-context-window-is-working-memory'))

The **context window** is everything the model can see at once: its instructions, the tool definitions and the whole conversation so far. What is left over is free space [[5]](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/context-windows).

[A typical mid-session breakdown of a coding agent's context. These colours mean the same thing in every figure that follows.](<$image.asset('fig-context-window.svg').alt('Stacked bar: system 12 percent, tools 18 percent, conversation 40 percent, free space 30 percent')>)

The system prompt and the tool definitions are sent with every request, and together they can take close to a third of the window before you type a word. The conversation grows every turn, mostly through tool calls and their results.

### [The model has no memory between calls]($heading.id('the-model-has-no-memory-between-calls'))

The model keeps nothing between requests. A "conversation" is a list of messages that the harness keeps and **sends again in full** on every request. Each answer is appended to the list and goes out with the next request.

[Every request carries the full history. The system prompt and tool definitions form a stable prefix.](<$image.asset('fig-stateless.svg').alt('Three requests, each longer than the last, showing earlier messages re-sent')>)

This has three consequences.

**Cost grows quadratically.** Say the stable prefix is [`P`]($mathtex) tokens and each turn adds [`d`]($mathtex) tokens. Request [`k`]($mathtex) then carries [`P + kd`]($mathtex) tokens, and after [`n`]($mathtex) turns you have sent

```=mathtex
\sum_{k=1}^{n} (P + kd) = nP + d\,\frac{n(n+1)}{2}
```

tokens in total, which grows as [`n^2`]($mathtex).

[Illustrative numbers: after 40 turns the harness has sent about 23 times more input tokens than the conversation contains.](<$image.asset('fig-quadratic.svg').alt('Line chart: cumulative tokens re-sent in full grow quadratically to 4.1 million after 40 turns; new tokens alone grow linearly to 180 thousand')>)

**Tool traffic dominates.** Tool calls and their results are messages too. A single test run or `grep` can return thousands of tokens, and those tokens then ride along on every later request.

**A stable prefix is cheaper.** Providers cache the unchanged start of a request, and a cached prefix is billed at a fraction of the normal input price [[6]](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching). The cache matches byte for byte, so an edited system prompt, a reordered tool list or a timestamp at the top invalidates it. Put stable content first and keep it fixed.

### [What one request looks like]($heading.id('what-one-request-looks-like'))

A request carries the tool definitions and a list of messages, each with a role:

| Role | What it holds |
|---|---|
| **system** | Persistent instructions: persona, rules, constraints. Sent first, every time |
| **user** | Your prompt |
| **assistant** | The model's earlier replies: text, and requests to call tools |
| **tool** | The result of a tool call, sent back by the harness |

Here is the request behind the opening example, slightly trimmed. It uses the OpenAI-compatible chat format that most providers accept, including Blablador, the LLM service at Jülich that the course uses:

```json
{
  "model": "alias-code",
  "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {
      "name": "read_file",
      "description": "Read a file from the repository.",
      "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path"],
                     "properties": {"path": {"type": "string"}}}}}],
  "messages": [
    {"role": "system", "content": "You are a coding assistant. Prefer small, tested changes."},
    {"role": "user", "content": "Why does test_auth fail?"},
    {"role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "c1", "type": "function",
        "function": {"name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\": \"tests/test_auth.py\"}"}}]},
    {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "c1", "content": "def test_login(client): ..."},
    {"role": "assistant", "content": "The fixture `client` is never defined ..."}
  ]
}
```

Read from the top, it holds the tool definition, the system prompt, your prompt, a tool call *from the model*, the result *from the harness* and the answer. All of it is sent again next turn. Anthropic's own API has the same parts, but puts the system prompt in a separate field and returns tool results inside a user message [[7]](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview). Either way, the model treats a tool result as something it observed, not as a request from you. That matters when a file or a web page contains text that looks like a command.

### [Advertised context is not usable context]($heading.id('advertised-context-is-not-usable-context'))

Model cards advertise context windows of 128K, 200K or a million tokens, but a model cannot use all of that equally well. The RULER benchmark [[8]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.06654) defines a model's **effective length** as the longest input at which it still scores above a fixed baseline on retrieval, multi-hop tracing and aggregation tasks. The long-context survey by Liu et al. [[9]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.17407) collects these numbers for many models:

[Claimed versus effective context length. Log scale, so every gridline is a factor of ten.](<$image.asset('fig-effective-context.svg').alt('Horizontal bar chart comparing claimed and effective context length for 17 models')>)

Most of these models hold up for only 12 to 50% of their advertised length, and some for under 2%. They are 2024 models, and newer ones do better, but the lesson stands: plan a long session around the effective number.

### [When the window fills up]($heading.id('when-the-window-fills-up'))

Quality drops well before the window is full, in three ways:

- **Lost in the middle.** Models use information at the start and the end of a long context better than information in the middle [[10]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172), so details in the middle get overlooked.
- **Diluted rules.** Conflicting instructions compete, and key requirements fade.
- **Autocompaction.** Near the limit, the harness summarizes the older turns, as described next.

[A practical threshold: when a session reaches half to 60% of the window, start a fresh one rather than wait for autocompaction.](<$image.asset('fig-window-fill.svg').alt('Context bar filled to about 55 percent, with a highlighted 50 to 60 percent band and an autocompaction marker near 92 percent')>)

### [Compaction: summarize, then drop]($heading.id('compaction-summarize-then-drop'))

When compaction runs, the harness replaces the older turns with a **summary**. The system prompt, the tool definitions and the most recent turns stay as they are.

[Before compaction, the window is full of old turns. After, a summary replaces them.](<$image.asset('fig-compaction.svg').alt('Two context bars: before, full of messages; after, system, tools, a summary block and a few recent messages')>)

| Survives | Lost |
|---|---|
| Facts the summary mentions | Exact output, error messages, line numbers |
| The most recent turns | Earlier reasoning and rejected options |
| Anything on disk | Constraints you stated only once |

The last row is the dangerous one. If a constraint matters, write it into `AGENTS.md` (section 4) or a file on disk, or state it again after compaction.

### [Cheaper levers than compaction]($heading.id('cheaper-levers-than-compaction'))

Compaction is the last resort. These keep the window small at less cost [[11]](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents):

- **Truncate output.** The harness caps long tool results, and you can filter yourself with `tail`, `grep` or `--quiet`.
- **Clear old results.** Keep a tool call but drop its bulky result once it has been used.
- **Delegate.** Run noisy work, such as reading dozens of files, in a subagent with its own context window, so that only its conclusion comes back [[12]](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents).
- **Persist to files.** Plans and notes on disk survive compaction.
- **Load fewer tools.** Every tool definition is sent with every request, so remove MCP servers you do not use.
- **Start fresh.** This is the only lossless option, because you choose what carries over. In Claude Code, `/clear` does it.

## [3. The loop: how does a model take action?]($heading.id('the-loop-how-does-a-model-take-action'))

### [Tools: the model asks, the harness acts]($heading.id('tools-the-model-asks-the-harness-acts'))

A model only produces text. It cannot run a test, read a file or edit code. **Tools** bridge that gap in three steps [[7]](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview).

**1. Definition.** With every request, the harness describes each tool by its name, a description and a JSON schema for its input:

```json
{"type": "function",
 "function": {
   "name": "bash",
   "description": "Run a shell command; run tests after every change.",
   "parameters": {"type": "object",
                  "properties": {"cmd": {"type": "string"}}}}}
```

**2. Call.** Instead of plain text, the model replies with a structured request to use a tool. The arguments arrive as a JSON string:

```json
{"role": "assistant",
 "tool_calls": [{"id": "c1", "type": "function",
   "function": {"name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"cmd\": \"pytest -x\"}"}}]}
```

**3. Result.** The harness runs the tool and sends the output back, tagged with the same id:

```json
{"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "c1",
 "content": "FAILED tests/test_auth.py::test_login\nfixture 'client' not found"}
```

The model **never runs anything**. It writes a request, and the harness runs it and returns the output as text. The description is also **a prompt**: "run tests after every change" in the definition above is an instruction, and the model will follow it. In a coding agent every action is a tool, typically `bash`, `read`, `edit`, `write`, `grep`, `glob`, `web fetch` and `ask user`.

### [The loop: call, run, append, repeat]($heading.id('the-loop-call-run-append-repeat'))

One tool call is a single step. Put it in a loop and you get an agent [[13]](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents).

[The harness calls the model, runs each tool call it gets back, appends the result and calls again.](<$image.asset('fig-agent-loop.svg').alt('You, harness and model, with a tool-call arrow looping back to the harness')>)

The harness sends the whole conversation to the model. If the reply contains a tool call, the harness runs it, appends the call and its result to the list, and calls the model again. The loop ends when a reply contains **no tool call**: the model has answered in plain text, and the harness passes that text back to you.

Replayed this way, the opening example is five calls to the model:

[One prompt, five model calls. Each call sends a longer list than the one before.](<$image.asset('fig-loop-replay.svg').alt('Five rows of growing message bars, each ending in a model call and its output')>)

Each tool call and its result join the next request, which is the quadratic growth from section 2 in action. Call 5 returns text, so there is nothing left to run and the loop stops.

### [A complete agent in a few lines]($heading.id('a-complete-agent-in-a-few-lines'))

The loop is small enough to write out in full. This is `examples/agent_loop_demo.py` from the course repository, condensed. It talks to Blablador through its Python client, and any OpenAI-compatible client works the same way:

```python
import json, subprocess
from blablador import Blablador
client = Blablador()  # reads BLABLADOR_API_KEY
TOOLS = [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "bash",
          "description": "Run a shell command and return its output.",
          "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["cmd"],
                         "properties": {"cmd": {"type": "string"}}}}}]
def run_bash(cmd):  # the tool: no checks, no limits
    p = subprocess.run(cmd, shell=True, capture_output=True, text=True)
    return p.stdout + p.stderr
messages = [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a coding agent."},
            {"role": "user", "content": input("> ")}]
while True:  # the agent loop
    msg = client.chat.completions.create(model="alias-fast", max_tokens=16000, tools=TOOLS,
                                         messages=messages).choices[0].message
    messages.append(msg.model_dump(exclude_none=True))
    if not msg.tool_calls:  # no tool requested: done
        print(msg.content); break
    for call in msg.tool_calls:  # one "tool" message per call
        messages.append({"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": call.id,
                         "content": run_bash(json.loads(call.function.arguments)["cmd"])})
```

Everything from the earlier sections is here: the tool definition, the `messages` list that is sent again on every call, the assistant reply with its tool calls, the results sent back as `tool` messages, and the check for an empty `tool_calls` that ends the loop.

It works, and it is also dangerous. It will run `rm -rf` if the model asks for it, it will hang on `npm start` because a dev server never exits, and it will pour a 40 MB log into the context. The version in the repository adds one safeguard against the last problem: it cuts tool output at 20,000 characters and tells the model that it did.

### [What a production harness adds]($heading.id('what-a-production-harness-adds'))

A production harness keeps the same short loop and adds the engineering around it:

| Concern | The script above | A real harness |
|---|---|---|
| Safety | Runs anything, as you | Allow and deny rules, approval prompts, a sandbox |
| Output size | Everything goes into the context | Truncated and paginated results |
| Time | Blocks until the command finishes | Timeouts, background jobs, cancellation |
| State | A new shell on every call | A persistent working directory and environment |
| Errors | An exception ends the loop | Exit code and stderr returned to the model |
| Descriptions | One sentence | Hundreds of words on when and how to use each tool |
| Cost | Nothing tracked | Token accounting, prompt caching, compaction |

## [4. The harness: where do you have leverage?]($heading.id('the-harness-where-do-you-have-leverage'))

### [Agent = model + harness]($heading.id('agent-model-harness'))

[The model is the engine. Everything around it is the harness.](<$image.asset('fig-harness.svg').alt('A model box at the centre surrounded by six harness components inside a dashed boundary')>)

An agent is a model plus a harness. The harness supplies the system prompt, the tools and the code that runs them, the loop, context management, permissions and the interface you work in. Claude Code, Codex CLI, Cursor, OpenCode and the script above are all harnesses. Put the same model in a different harness and you get a different agent.

The harness matters for three reasons:

1. Most of what you experience as "the agent" is harness behaviour: which files it reads first, when it asks permission, how it recovers from a failed test and what it forgets.
2. Agent benchmarks score a model together with its harness, and changing the harness moves the score. The SWE-agent paper [[14]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.15793) showed that the same model solved far more real GitHub issues when given better tools, output formats and guardrails.
3. A model upgrade changes the engine, while a harness change changes the driving. You rarely choose the model's weights, but you change the harness all the time.

### [Four levers you control]($heading.id('four-levers-you-control'))

| Lever | What it does | Cost in context |
|---|---|---|
| **AGENTS.md** | Tells the agent what it should always know | Every request, in full |
| **Skills** | Package a workflow once and load it when needed | A name and description, until used |
| **MCP** | Connects new tools and data sources through one protocol | Every tool definition, every request |
| **Hooks** | Run deterministic checks at fixed points in the loop | None |

The examples below use Claude Code, but most harnesses have equivalents.

### [AGENTS.md: instructions loaded every time]($heading.id('agents-md-instructions-loaded-every-time'))

`AGENTS.md` is a project file that the harness injects into the system prompt on every request. It is the place for what the agent should always know:

```md
# orders-api

## Commands
- Test: uv run pytest -x
- Lint: uv run ruff check --fix

## Conventions
- Python 3.12, type hints everywhere
- Business logic in src/services/
- Never edit files under vendor/
```

`AGENTS.md` is an open format that many tools read [[15]](https://agents.md). Claude Code reads `CLAUDE.md` instead [[16]](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory). Treat the file like any other configuration: keep it under version control, keep it short because every request pays for every line, put the most important rules first, and prune instructions that no longer apply.

### [Skills: reusable workflows, loaded on demand]($heading.id('skills-reusable-workflows-loaded-on-demand'))

A skill is a folder with a `SKILL.md` file, which holds instructions and can come with scripts and reference files [[17]](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills). This one lives in `~/.claude/skills/explain-code/SKILL.md`:

```md
---
name: explain-code
description: Explains code with diagrams
user-invocable: true
allowed-tools: Read, Grep, Glob
---
When explaining code:
1. Draw a diagram: ASCII art
2. Walk through: step-by-step
3. Highlight a gotcha
```

Skills are **cheap until used**. Only the name and the description sit in the context, and the body is loaded when the skill runs. `AGENTS.md`, in contrast, is paid for on every request. You can run a skill yourself as a slash command, such as `/explain-code src/auth/middleware.ts`, or let the model pick it from its description.

### [MCP: the Model Context Protocol]($heading.id('mcp-the-model-context-protocol'))

The Model Context Protocol (MCP) [[18]](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) is an open standard for giving agents new tools. An MCP server is a small program that offers tools, and it works with any agent that speaks MCP.

[Many clients, many servers, one protocol in between.](<$image.asset('fig-mcp.svg').alt('Four MCP clients on the left connected through a central MCP hub to five servers on the right')>)

For example, jobstate is an MCP server that lets an agent check, submit and watch Slurm jobs on the JSC clusters. Adding it to Claude Code takes two commands [[19]](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp):

```sh
pip install jobstate
claude mcp add jobstate -- jobstate-mcp
```

To the model, MCP tools are ordinary tools. The course's `examples/agent_mcp_demo.py` shows this by running the loop from section 3 against jobstate's server instead of a `bash` tool. It asks the server for its tools, turns each one into a tool definition, and forwards every tool call to the server. Condensed, the bridge looks like this:

```python
tools = (await mcp.list_tools()).tools
tool_defs = [{"type": "function",
              "function": {"name": t.name, "description": t.description,
                           "parameters": t.input_schema}}
             for t in tools]
...
for call in reply.tool_calls:
    result = await mcp.call_tool(call.function.name, json.loads(call.function.arguments))
    messages.append({"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": call.id,
                     "content": "\n".join(c.text for c in result.content)})
```

With that bridge, a model on Blablador summarizes the GPU partitions, submits a short job after a dry run, waits until the job's log shows progress and reports how the job ended.

The catch is cost: **every server's tool definitions go out with every request**. Section 2 showed that tools can already fill a fifth of the window, so add only the servers you use.

### [Hooks: rules that always run]($heading.id('hooks-rules-that-always-run'))

Hooks are shell commands that the harness runs at fixed points in the loop [[20]](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks). The model can overlook an instruction in `AGENTS.md`, but a hook runs every time its event fires.

[Hook events sit at fixed points of the loop. Your command runs there every time.](<$image.asset('fig-hooks.svg').alt('Timeline from model picks a tool, through PreToolUse, tool runs, PostToolUse, Notification and Stop hook events')>)

- **PreToolUse** runs before a tool executes, for example to block dangerous commands.
- **PostToolUse** runs after a tool executes, for example to format edited files.
- **Notification** alerts you when the agent needs input.
- **Stop** runs when the agent wants to finish, for example to check that the tests pass.

This `PostToolUse` hook in `.claude/settings.json` runs `ruff format` on every file the agent edits or writes. The hook receives the tool call as JSON on stdin:

```json
{"hooks": {"PostToolUse": [{"matcher": "Edit|Write",
  "hooks": [{"type": "command",
    "command": "jq -r '.tool_input.file_path' | xargs ruff format"}]}]}}
```

With it in place, formatting no longer depends on the model remembering to do it.

## [5. What to take home]($heading.id('what-to-take-home'))

1. **The model predicts one token at a time.** Sampling settings matter less than a clear prompt, and coding tools set them for you.
2. **Context is the scarce resource.** Everything is sent again on every request, quality drops well before the window is full, and starting fresh is the only lossless reset.
3. **The loop is a few lines.** Call the model, run its tool calls, append the results, and repeat until it answers in text.
4. **The harness is where you have leverage.** `AGENTS.md`, skills, MCP servers and hooks change what the agent knows, can do and must do.

The course continues with a hands-on exercise in which you use a coding agent to compute building surface areas from real 3D building data. The exercise, the slides and the example scripts are in the [course repository](https://gitlab.jsc.fz-juelich.de/sdlaml/agentic-coding/agentic-coding-workflows), and more guides are on the [SDL Applied ML AI pages](https://sdlaml.pages.jsc.fz-juelich.de/ai/).

